# Dates from engine/notation/chronicle.ts: the two world wars (1914 to 1918, 1939 to 1945), British
# evacuation 1939, Anne Frank's diary 1942 to 1944, the UN 1945, rationing's end in Britain 1954.
# The ration sums use round numbers made up for them.
lesson history-the-twentieth-century-through-families v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=4 subject=history levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The twentieth century through families"
  goal "Put the century's great events in order, and say from diaries and letters what they meant for families at home."

  look {
    scene 36x12 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 0) space=2 align=start {
        houses row count=2 windows=2
        sourcecard card title="Anne Frank's diary" kind=3 who="Anne Frank, a girl in hiding" when="1942 to 1944" where="Amsterdam" there=1 ask=0
      }
    }
    say """
      Twice in the twentieth century great wars spread across the world, from 1914 to 1918 and from
      1939 to 1945. For families at home they meant food shared out by rationing, children sent away
      from cities, fathers and mothers far away, and letters waited for. Some children kept diaries,
      like Anne Frank, who wrote while her family hid in Amsterdam. After 1945 the countries of the
      world set up the United Nations to keep the peace.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.wartime-home k=0
    }
    show history.wartime-home k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show history.c20-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.ration-sums k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.wartime-diary k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.wartime-home k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show history.c20-order k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.ration-sums k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show history.wartime-diary k=1 hard-k=0
    show history.source-gap k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    level hard {
      show history.c20-order k=3 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.why-diaries k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.letter-home
  }
  remember {
    say "The century's wars reached every home: rationing, children sent away, and letters. Diaries kept at the time let us see it."
  }

  grown-ups """
    This lesson touches war, and it is told through homes and children, not battles: rationing,
    evacuation, letters and a diary. The idea is that great events are lived by ordinary families,
    and that sources made at the time (a child's diary, letters home) let us see it. The way in
    asks why food was rationed; the core orders the century's events, shares out a ration, and
    finds the first-hand source. Anne Frank died in 1945 in a camp; the lesson does not tell that,
    and you may choose when to. If your family has its own stories of those years, this is a good
    time for them. The three-star task is for you to mark: a letter home as an evacuated child.
    England's key stage 2 history allows "a significant turning point in British history, for
    example ... the Battle of Britain"; Japan's 社会 for grade six reaches the war and the post-war
    years through the lives of people (「我が国の歴史上の主な事象について，人物の働きや代表的な文化遺産を中心に」,
    the course of study as read in its 2008 edition); Russia's history programme teaches the
    twentieth century in its later grades, and families there keep 9 May as a day of memory.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says why food was rationed, the events are far apart, the rations are for one week, and the diary is the first card."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the home-front reasons are about letters and ration books, the events are close together, the rations run to four weeks, the diary is the second card, and the two-star question compares the diary with a history book."
  }
}
